 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** Training takes up to 30 minutes per scene on a single RTX 3090 GPU [S1].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Performance:** The configuration is tuned for CO3D (18,618 scenes) and ScanNet (1,513 scenes, 16 excluded for blur) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes training on object-centric and indoor data; reduces floaters in ScanNet via depth initialization; improves sparsity control via higher lambda [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires ground truth depth for ScanNet initialization; excludes scenes with motion blur [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When training Plenoxels on CO3D or ScanNet datasets using the PeRFception framework.
-*   **When not to use:** When training on datasets without depth maps (ScanNet-specific initialization fails) or when computational budget is extremely limited (30 mins/scene is higher than some real-time methods).
+*   **Performance:** The configuration is tuned for the CO3D (18,618 scenes) and ScanNet (1,513 scenes, 16 excluded) datasets [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes training via warm-up and specific learning rates; handles ScanNet's noisy depth data through robust outlier filtering; improves sparsity control via increased sparsity loss weight [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Resolution does not directly correlate with average rendering quality on ScanNet (tested at 128, 256, 512) [S1]. Fails to render unobserved parts reliably if camera poses are too distant from training views [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When training Plenoxels on CO3D or ScanNet datasets where standard configurations fail to converge or produce artifacts due to sparse data or motion blur [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The PeRFception framework was introduced in the paper "PeRFception: Perception using Radiance Fields" [S1]. It adapts the Plenoxels method [14] for perception tasks on CO3D and ScanNet datasets. The configuration addresses specific challenges such as motion blur, sparse observation, and floaters in empty space [S1].
+The PeRFception variants were introduced in the PeRFception paper [S1]. The framework builds on the Plenoxels method [S1], which uses PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels [S1]. The specific configurations for CO3D and ScanNet were developed to address the unique characteristics of these datasets, such as object-centric variability in CO3D and indoor scene sparsity/noise in ScanNet [S1].
 
